+---
+id: dl-001-resolvepointer-strict-rfc-6901-semantics-throws-on-any-non-resolution
+type: decision-log
+title: "resolvePointer: strict RFC 6901 semantics, throws on any non-resolution"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+<!-- decision-log body. `wingfoil memory add` copies this scaffold, setting only `id`, `title` and
+     `status` (and any `--tags` or `--set` value). Fill the required frontmatter fields and replace
+     these placeholder comments yourself; `wingfoil memory submit` then checks the required fields,
+     moves `status` forward and commits the document as you left it, naming in the commit body the
+     content it carries. -->
+
+## Context
+Other services will build on `resolvePointer`, so it must follow RFC 6901 exactly.
+
+## Decision
+- `""` returns the whole document; any other pointer must start with `/`.
+- Only `~0` and `~1` escapes are valid (decoded `~1` first, then `~0`); any other `~` is an error.
+- Array tokens must match `0|[1-9][0-9]*` and be within bounds; `-` (past-the-end) throws, as it
+  refers to no value.
+- Object members are looked up as own properties only, so `__proto__`, `constructor` etc. do not
+  resolve unless actually present in the document.
+- Descending into a scalar or `null`, or a missing member, throws `Error`. A non-string pointer
+  throws `TypeError`.
+- The URI-fragment form (`#/...`) is not supported; it is a different representation.
+
+## Consequences
+Callers can rely on a thrown error meaning "invalid or unresolvable"; they cannot distinguish the two
+by type. Later features (e.g. set/patch) may need to reuse the token parsing, which can be extracted then.
